      "paragraph_source_identity": {
        "path": "C:\\Users\\alver\\.codex\\.chatgpt-projects\\g-p-6aa4088af61c8191a4b578a4310457ae\\.prof-sasis\\20261014-d008-r40\\earlier-browser-boundary-source-packets\\D007-P5-explanation.paragraph-source.txt",
        "bytes": 607,
        "sha256": "fce78eb744ac918705095853edd7aefb00c3ae78ae3a7e72e6a1d9d9a7fd3591"
      },
      "paragraph_source": "Equivalently, differentiating $`e^{x\\ln x}`$ by the chain and product rules gives the same expression. The constant-power rule assumed the exponent's derivative was zero. Here the exponent is $`x`$, whose derivative is 1, and its variation creates the $`\\ln x`$ term. Substituting $`r=x`$ into a rule derived for fixed $`r`$ changes that assumption. A check only at $`x=1`$ would miss this mistake because $`\\ln1=0`$; at $`x=e`$ the correct derivative is $`2e^e`$, which distinguishes it from the incorrectly substituted constant-power result $`e^e`$. [Return to P5](lesson.md#p5) · [Hint P5](hints.md#h5)\n",
      "immutable_browser_url": "https://github.com/alverstris/PROF-Skill.md-/blob/4765d39aa399a0a1ff09223cae21b15ce6c7f720/runs/y1-reader-20261008/iterations/007/published-r29-20261011/teaching/solutions.md",
